WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.743
VGGT stacks twenty-four pairs of different blocks to rebuild a scene from photos.

00:00:04.743 --> 00:00:08.659
What if one pair, run again and again, did the same job?

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:12.160
Hi, I'm Oswin!

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.928
Déjà View rebuilds a scene by running the same block over and over.

00:00:15.928 --> 00:00:17.088
Let's open it up.

00:00:19.842 --> 00:00:20.631
Chapter one.

00:00:20.631 --> 00:00:22.210
Photos in, geometry out.

00:00:26.342 --> 00:00:28.698
Hand the model photos from different angles.

00:00:28.698 --> 00:00:31.391
An encoder turns each one into patch tokens.

00:00:31.811 --> 00:00:35.257
VGGT pushes them through twenty-four pairs of different blocks.

00:00:35.257 --> 00:00:40.236
Frame attention looks within one photo; global attention looks across all of them.

00:00:40.656 --> 00:00:43.402
Heads read out depth maps, point maps and cameras.

00:00:43.856 --> 00:00:48.595
Accuracy grew with size: VGGT has one point two six billion parameters.

00:00:50.342 --> 00:00:52.457
Here is the job, from NVIDIA's post.

00:00:52.457 --> 00:00:56.386
Drone footage goes in; a point cloud and the camera's path come out.

00:00:57.842 --> 00:00:58.675
Chapter two.

00:00:58.675 --> 00:01:00.341
One block, sixteen times.

00:01:03.642 --> 00:01:06.962
Deep stacks seem to refine their answer layer by layer.

00:01:06.962 --> 00:01:11.610
So make that explicit: one shared block, applied K times to the same tokens.

00:01:13.342 --> 00:01:14.375
The paper's diagram.

00:01:14.375 --> 00:01:18.164
Encoder tokens on the left, two shallow decoders on the right.

00:01:18.584 --> 00:01:22.166
In the middle, one block, frame then global attention, fed back

00:01:22.166 --> 00:01:23.468
into itself K times.

00:01:25.492 --> 00:01:28.889
Each loop reads the whole state, every token of every view, and

00:01:28.889 --> 00:01:30.022
writes a refined one.

00:01:30.442 --> 00:01:34.100
Decode it after two loops: the room is smeared, the cameras far off.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.464
After four, the walls start to line up.

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:40.034
After eight, the cameras are close.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:44.905
After sixteen, sharp geometry and accurate cameras.

00:01:44.905 --> 00:01:47.690
Training only ever checks the last step.

00:01:49.192 --> 00:01:51.649
Inside, an ordinary block with three dials.

00:01:52.069 --> 00:01:55.539
Attention, then an MLP, each added with a scale per channel.

00:01:55.959 --> 00:01:59.220
The scales come from a tiny network that reads where this step

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:00.579
starts and ends in time.

00:02:00.579 --> 00:02:04.112
That is how one block tells an early step from a late one.

00:02:04.532 --> 00:02:06.543
Then a state gate scales everything.

00:02:06.543 --> 00:02:10.565
In the released weights it sits below one, so older content fades.

00:02:12.592 --> 00:02:14.470
I counted the checkpoint's tensors.

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:19.352
Three quarters is the encoder, eighty-six point six million, run once per photo.

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:23.123
The block doing the multi-view reasoning is fourteen and a half

00:02:23.123 --> 00:02:24.342
million, run sixteen times.

00:02:24.762 --> 00:02:27.064
Two shallow decoders read out depth and rays.

00:02:27.702 --> 00:02:30.173
One hundred and seventeen million in all.

00:02:30.173 --> 00:02:34.410
Untied, sixteen copies would make it about three hundred and twenty-nine million.

00:02:36.342 --> 00:02:37.024
Chapter three.

00:02:37.024 --> 00:02:38.046
Turning the dial.

00:02:42.692 --> 00:02:45.673
One checkpoint serves every loop count from eight to sixteen.

00:02:46.093 --> 00:02:50.096
Each batch draws a loop count between eight and sixteen, favouring the high end.

00:02:50.516 --> 00:02:53.676
The gates read time, not a step number, so one set of

00:02:53.676 --> 00:02:54.729
weights fits every count.

00:02:55.149 --> 00:02:56.766
Only the final state is scored.

00:02:59.692 --> 00:03:00.715
Want fewer loops?

00:03:00.715 --> 00:03:03.102
You could stop the sixteen-step schedule early.

00:03:03.522 --> 00:03:06.095
Or run K steps on a K-step time grid.

00:03:06.095 --> 00:03:07.525
That wins at every budget.

00:03:07.945 --> 00:03:11.796
At eight loops, pose AUC at three degrees is zero point four

00:03:11.796 --> 00:03:14.684
three, against zero point three one for stopping early.

00:03:16.092 --> 00:03:17.128
Now the bill.

00:03:17.128 --> 00:03:21.270
Ten times fewer parameters is not ten times fewer FLOPs: half or

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:25.758
less of Pi3 and VGGT, about the same as Depth Anything 3 Large.

00:03:27.592 --> 00:03:28.582
Why so close?

00:03:28.582 --> 00:03:29.572
Count one loop.

00:03:29.572 --> 00:03:31.881
The linear layers cost under one teraflop.

00:03:32.301 --> 00:03:35.880
Frame attention is cheap: each photo sees only its own tokens.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:40.092
Global attention is three of the four teraflops, every token looking

00:03:40.092 --> 00:03:41.471
at all thirty-one thousand.

00:03:41.471 --> 00:03:43.540
Sharing weights does not touch that.

00:03:45.342 --> 00:03:45.891
Chapter four.

00:03:45.891 --> 00:03:46.988
Does it hold up?

00:03:51.842 --> 00:03:55.609
A sign the loop does real work: one patch's global attention onto

00:03:55.609 --> 00:03:57.179
another view, loop by loop.

00:03:57.599 --> 00:04:01.021
It starts diffuse and settles on the matching point, though training

00:04:01.021 --> 00:04:02.266
never asked for matches.

00:04:03.842 --> 00:04:04.869
And the limit.

00:04:04.869 --> 00:04:08.979
Past sixteen loops the state drifts; past about thirty steps, it collapses.

00:04:10.692 --> 00:04:12.186
What I found checking the claims.

00:04:12.725 --> 00:04:17.233
It leads the averages, an inlier ratio of eighty point three, but

00:04:17.233 --> 00:04:20.614
wins few single cells against the concurrent VGGT Omega.

00:04:21.034 --> 00:04:24.012
Shared beat untied blocks, but only in half-length runs.

00:04:24.432 --> 00:04:28.135
And the quick-start script runs twelve loops; the tables use sixteen.

00:04:29.642 --> 00:04:31.651
Tying weights shrinks parameters, not FLOPs.

00:04:31.651 --> 00:04:36.339
And the loop count is a real dial, above all for precise camera poses.

00:04:37.692 --> 00:04:38.440
To recap.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:39.935
One shared block, looped.

00:04:39.935 --> 00:04:41.804
Most parameters are the encoder.

00:04:41.804 --> 00:04:44.421
For fewer loops, re-grid; don't stop early.

00:04:44.841 --> 00:04:46.907
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:46.907 --> 00:04:47.497
I'm Oswin.

00:04:47.497 --> 00:04:47.792
Bye!
